WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:03.470
- Checking connection, you are now live.

00:00:03.470 --> 00:00:05.120
Hello!

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:06.540
We are telling your followers

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:08.240
that you started a live video.

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:09.456
I just tried it.

00:00:09.456 --> 00:00:10.366
You're so kind.

00:00:10.366 --> 00:00:11.310
(Kelly laughs)

00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:13.968
Hello! I'm in between shows.

00:00:13.968 --> 00:00:16.322
Oh, what's up Tammy?

00:00:16.322 --> 00:00:18.050
I am in between shows right now

00:00:18.050 --> 00:00:20.270
and I thought we'd do an Instagram Live

00:00:20.270 --> 00:00:21.510
for y'all.

00:00:21.510 --> 00:00:22.500
I didn't think that

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:23.840
somebody else thought it would be cool

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:24.673
and I'm not cool

00:00:24.673 --> 00:00:25.670
and I have to think of
something like that,

00:00:25.670 --> 00:00:26.660
let's just be real,

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:28.140
but I was like, "Yeah, let's do it."

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:30.270
So anyway, happy new year everybody,

00:00:30.270 --> 00:00:33.079
this is our first Instagram Live of 2022

00:00:33.079 --> 00:00:36.160
and happy new year y'all,

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:38.710
I hope you all are having
a great year so far.

00:00:38.710 --> 00:00:40.473
I know some things haven't changed,

00:00:41.890 --> 00:00:44.180
global pandemic, but we're working on it

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:46.395
I think as human race, we wanna get there.

00:00:46.395 --> 00:00:50.593
Anyway, there's a surge
happening now in LA

00:00:50.593 --> 00:00:52.680
and specifically I know,

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:55.410
so we have no audience right now,

00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:57.560
it's all virtual for a minute.

00:00:57.560 --> 00:00:59.960
Again, but we do have
left guests, which is fine

00:01:01.236 --> 00:01:02.069
and we're keeping it safe,

00:01:02.069 --> 00:01:04.700
so we wanted to keep all our people safe,

00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:06.020
so that's why we did that,

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:07.370
but anyway, just wanna say happy new year

00:01:07.370 --> 00:01:09.020
and I hear that we are almost,

00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:10.830
I heard a million followers,

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:13.590
we're almost a million
followers, so come on,

00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:16.963
we all know 6,000 people.(laughs)

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:20.640
Just call them.(laughs)

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:21.840
- [Tricia] A lot of you.

00:01:22.775 --> 00:01:25.520
- If one of you calls 6,000 people,

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:28.373
we can do this.(laughs)

00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:32.520
We're a little tired,
so this should go well

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:33.809
and we have no one here,
it's just me and Tricia

00:01:33.809 --> 00:01:37.571
who are so not good at stuff like that,

00:01:37.571 --> 00:01:38.980
such a party.

00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:40.190
Anyway, so I think

00:01:40.190 --> 00:01:41.870
I'm gonna be answering questions, right?

00:01:41.870 --> 00:01:42.703
- Yes.
- I'm answering questions,

00:01:42.703 --> 00:01:43.867
you've so many questions

00:01:43.867 --> 00:01:45.730
and I'm going to answer them.

00:01:45.730 --> 00:01:47.380
- [Tricia] Gracie wants to know,

00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:49.060
what is a typical day schedule like

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:50.550
when you're filming "The
Kelly Clarkson Show"

00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:51.793
from makeup to sleep?

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:55.760
- Oh, well I'll tell
you about my day today.

00:01:55.760 --> 00:01:57.560
I let my kids have a sleepover last night

00:01:57.560 --> 00:02:00.580
because I was so tired.

00:02:00.580 --> 00:02:02.170
Listen, I'm one of those tools

00:02:02.170 --> 00:02:03.660
that's like, "Man, you're no man."

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:04.780
and I'll start to work it out

00:02:04.780 --> 00:02:07.650
and I also started working again

00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:08.670
like after having,

00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:10.150
the longest period I've had off,

00:02:10.150 --> 00:02:11.630
I feel like in 20 years,

00:02:11.630 --> 00:02:13.590
which was like 20 days maybe.

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:15.830
So most of that was with kids,

00:02:15.830 --> 00:02:16.930
so not super often

00:02:16.930 --> 00:02:18.950
but it was not working

00:02:18.950 --> 00:02:19.887
and so we got back to work

00:02:19.887 --> 00:02:20.720
and all of us were like,

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:22.250
"Oh my gosh, is everyone really tired?"

00:02:22.250 --> 00:02:23.360
And then pluses are working out.

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:25.820
So I was really tired
and I was literally like,

00:02:25.820 --> 00:02:27.650
I couldn't even go up
the stairs to my kids,

00:02:27.650 --> 00:02:28.990
but I was like, "You
know, it's going to be fun

00:02:28.990 --> 00:02:29.823
if we have a sleep over."

00:02:29.823 --> 00:02:30.660
And they love sleepovers

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:32.930
and I don't allow it unless it's weekends,

00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:34.910
but mama was tired, I couldn't do it.

00:02:34.910 --> 00:02:36.460
I couldn't do the whole
routine, I couldn't.

00:02:36.460 --> 00:02:37.860
So I let them sleep with me

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:39.090
and they were so excited.

00:02:39.090 --> 00:02:40.820
So I woke up this morning

00:02:40.820 --> 00:02:43.760
to my five and seven year old

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:44.890
giggling and laughing.

00:02:44.890 --> 00:02:45.723
It was wonderful.

00:02:45.723 --> 00:02:49.430
And we all were cuddling and hugging.

00:02:49.430 --> 00:02:51.180
And then I said, "Okay,
we have to get up."

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:53.070
Cause we were already 20 minutes late.

00:02:53.070 --> 00:02:54.463
And I know my nanny was like,

00:02:54.463 --> 00:02:56.267
"We got to start at some point."

00:02:57.700 --> 00:02:59.403
So she has three kids sometimes.

00:03:00.650 --> 00:03:01.483
So anyways,

00:03:01.483 --> 00:03:02.380
so we got the kids up

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:04.407
and she helped me with
the kids this morning

00:03:04.407 --> 00:03:06.900
and while I was getting ready

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:08.170
and then I had to get to work

00:03:08.170 --> 00:03:10.390
a little bit earlier
because we actually have,

00:03:10.390 --> 00:03:12.460
today is an odd day.

00:03:12.460 --> 00:03:14.000
My music director,

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:15.100
I feel like he has never taken a day off

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:15.933
in his whole life.

00:03:15.933 --> 00:03:17.420
He had just some family stuff come up.

00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:19.670
So we had to go and help his family.

00:03:19.670 --> 00:03:21.060
And so we have touch,

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:22.570
I filling in as our musical director.

00:03:22.570 --> 00:03:24.810
So were here just a tiny bit early

00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:25.643
to go through the show

00:03:25.643 --> 00:03:26.580
and everyone's kind of getting back.

00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:27.870
And then we read scripts.

00:03:27.870 --> 00:03:30.180
I read scripts with
Alex executive producer.

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:31.180
Well, I read them the night before,

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:32.760
but I mean, we just kind
of go through the show

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:34.230
and how it's going to go.

00:03:34.230 --> 00:03:35.590
And if there are any questions,

00:03:35.590 --> 00:03:36.930
usually this is the time

00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:39.673
when I don't how to
pronounce someone's name.

00:03:40.673 --> 00:03:45.220
And then usually we do
rehearsals for songs,

00:03:45.220 --> 00:03:46.053
but we haven't been doing that.

00:03:46.053 --> 00:03:48.410
I've just been doing it
right before I sing them.

00:03:48.410 --> 00:03:50.130
So then we go down and
we do the show I sing

00:03:50.130 --> 00:03:50.963
and then we do the show

00:03:50.963 --> 00:03:52.390
and then we have a break.

00:03:52.390 --> 00:03:53.810
And sometimes I'm doing stuff like this

00:03:53.810 --> 00:03:55.500
in my break right after lunch.

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:57.010
And then we do a whole other show.

00:03:57.010 --> 00:03:58.230
We should do two shows a day.

00:03:58.230 --> 00:04:01.530
We always not usually, we always what?

00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:02.540
Wrong word.

00:04:02.540 --> 00:04:04.093
We always do two shows a day.

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:08.240
And then we finish up,

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:09.350
we do the same kind of thing again

00:04:09.350 --> 00:04:10.910
with a different show, different people.

00:04:10.910 --> 00:04:12.380
And that's the cool thing about it.

00:04:12.380 --> 00:04:14.810
It's kinda like musical theater

00:04:14.810 --> 00:04:16.830
in the sense of every show is different.

00:04:16.830 --> 00:04:18.200
I mean, it's not the
same script obviously,

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:20.340
but I just mean every same people

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:21.173
and same kind of dynamics,

00:04:21.173 --> 00:04:23.780
but it's like different
live setting every time.

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:25.950
So it's kind of fun in that sense.

00:04:25.950 --> 00:04:27.905
And you know, depending on the day,

00:04:27.905 --> 00:04:31.640
karaokes go well, or they don't,

00:04:31.640 --> 00:04:33.110
it's literally, people don't get it.

00:04:33.110 --> 00:04:36.080
I'm literally learning the song

00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:37.630
right before I sing it.

00:04:37.630 --> 00:04:39.640
So if they're not like staples,

00:04:39.640 --> 00:04:40.670
like always on my mind,

00:04:40.670 --> 00:04:41.750
Willie Nelson, something like that.

00:04:41.750 --> 00:04:43.614
But so yeah,

00:04:43.614 --> 00:04:45.200
that happens pretty quickly.

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:46.052
And then I go home

00:04:46.052 --> 00:04:47.980
and then I hang with my kids.

00:04:47.980 --> 00:04:49.860
And last night we did puzzles.

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:51.540
My son is into puzzles

00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:54.160
and my daughter is into right now,

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:56.210
saying that everything we do is not cool.

00:04:58.750 --> 00:05:00.690
And we were trying to
get Mario brothers work.

00:05:00.690 --> 00:05:02.390
I couldn't because Tricia's the one

00:05:02.390 --> 00:05:03.240
that knows how to build stuff

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:04.120
and I couldn't get it,

00:05:04.120 --> 00:05:06.110
technology is not my friend.

00:05:06.110 --> 00:05:08.090
So anyway, and that's it.

00:05:08.090 --> 00:05:08.923
And then, yeah,

00:05:08.923 --> 00:05:11.300
we'll end up probably snuffing again,

00:05:11.300 --> 00:05:12.493
cause I'm so tired.

00:05:13.700 --> 00:05:16.180
Working out is exhausting

00:05:16.180 --> 00:05:19.080
if you don't do it normally.

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:20.217
And then all of a sudden you're like,

00:05:20.217 --> 00:05:21.920
"It's January, I'm gonna be that tool

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:23.320
that works out for a month?"

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:24.993
Yeah.

00:05:25.990 --> 00:05:28.090
It's like an 18 minute workout too.

00:05:28.090 --> 00:05:30.705
It's an 18-minute workout Tricia.

00:05:30.705 --> 00:05:31.538
You know what?

00:05:31.538 --> 00:05:32.371
You do the 18 minutes.

00:05:32.371 --> 00:05:33.204
Tricia's gonna do 18 minutes.

00:05:33.204 --> 00:05:34.610
- [Tricia] I have time
for about 15 minutes.

00:05:34.610 --> 00:05:36.560
- Okay. It's circuits

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:40.530
and it's hard if you got to...

00:05:40.530 --> 00:05:42.929
Becky, I don't know
how I can talk on here.

00:05:42.929 --> 00:05:43.762
I don't know what

00:05:43.762 --> 00:05:44.810
you're allowed to say on here, so.

00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:46.880
- [Tricia] Okay, question number two.

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:48.780
- Okay.
- [Tricia] Joan Suzanne,

00:05:48.780 --> 00:05:50.171
what song do you wish

00:05:50.171 --> 00:05:51.863
you had written from another artist?

00:05:53.320 --> 00:05:54.153
- Plenty.

00:05:54.153 --> 00:05:56.530
Oh my God! There's so many songs I love.

00:05:56.530 --> 00:05:57.380
So I mean,

00:05:57.380 --> 00:06:01.420
my go-to is probably
"Why" by any Any Links.

00:06:01.420 --> 00:06:04.490
I just fell in love
with that song as a kid,

00:06:04.490 --> 00:06:07.240
I love it and I love how it's written.

00:06:07.240 --> 00:06:08.870
It's not formulaic

00:06:08.870 --> 00:06:11.353
like how most songs are written.

00:06:12.550 --> 00:06:14.090
That's probably,

00:06:14.090 --> 00:06:17.464
I mean, there's a lot
like any YouTube song,

00:06:17.464 --> 00:06:18.297
you know what I'm saying?

00:06:18.297 --> 00:06:19.130
There's like a lot of songs

00:06:19.130 --> 00:06:20.373
that you wish you'd written.

00:06:21.890 --> 00:06:22.970
Yeah, I should've been more prepared

00:06:22.970 --> 00:06:23.870
for that question.

00:06:25.050 --> 00:06:25.883
- [Tricia] This is a good one.

00:06:25.883 --> 00:06:26.716
Aaron wants to know.

00:06:26.716 --> 00:06:28.970
Would you ever rerecord
some of your older hits

00:06:28.970 --> 00:06:31.840
with your current sound and frame of mind?

00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:32.673
- Yes.

00:06:32.673 --> 00:06:33.506
And you know why?

00:06:33.506 --> 00:06:35.270
It's not really cause a friend of mine

00:06:35.270 --> 00:06:36.103
or anything,

00:06:36.103 --> 00:06:38.400
it's because I feel like I was tweeting

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:39.570
and I feel like it was Tailor Swift

00:06:39.570 --> 00:06:41.540
cause she wouldn't want
to redo or whatever.

00:06:41.540 --> 00:06:42.373
And I was like,

00:06:42.373 --> 00:06:43.403
"You should just recut them all."

00:06:43.403 --> 00:06:44.580
And I think what's
interesting about doing that

00:06:44.580 --> 00:06:45.413
later in life is,

00:06:45.413 --> 00:06:47.500
one, I personally feel like

00:06:47.500 --> 00:06:50.920
my voice is stronger than
it was when I was 19.

00:06:50.920 --> 00:06:52.620
It should be, I mean, I hope so.

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:55.130
Like after working hard for 20 years

00:06:55.130 --> 00:06:57.330
and it should've gotten a little better,

00:06:57.330 --> 00:06:59.550
so I feel like my range is bigger

00:06:59.550 --> 00:07:02.380
and it's just a different voice.

00:07:02.380 --> 00:07:06.760
So I think it would be
interesting to rerecord

00:07:06.760 --> 00:07:08.240
some of those songs that I love

00:07:08.240 --> 00:07:09.073
or even some of the hits

00:07:09.073 --> 00:07:10.670
that I actually can't listen to

00:07:10.670 --> 00:07:12.870
because sometimes also,

00:07:12.870 --> 00:07:15.544
look, I'm just trying to sometimes,

00:07:15.544 --> 00:07:16.377
like sometimes the producer

00:07:16.377 --> 00:07:17.240
keeps you there so long

00:07:18.471 --> 00:07:21.370
and 99% of the time

00:07:21.370 --> 00:07:23.680
get along with all producers and humanity,

00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:26.017
but there's a 1% where I'm just like,

00:07:26.017 --> 00:07:29.280
"Oh, you're not going to
let me leave this studio

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:31.380
until I sing it exactly
how you're wanting to.

00:07:31.380 --> 00:07:34.900
like with a ye ho, instead of you."

00:07:34.900 --> 00:07:36.390
Or things like that,

00:07:36.390 --> 00:07:38.550
that I can't listen to my own music

00:07:40.913 --> 00:07:42.680
because it's just weird.

00:07:42.680 --> 00:07:44.220
So I would like to,

00:07:44.220 --> 00:07:45.770
I think that would be fun to do,

00:07:45.770 --> 00:07:46.603
like at this stage of my life,

00:07:46.603 --> 00:07:47.590
with my voice now,

00:07:47.590 --> 00:07:49.220
I think that would be really cool.

00:07:49.220 --> 00:07:51.330
- [Tricia] Yeah. Heather wants to know,

00:07:51.330 --> 00:07:52.940
what has been one of your favorite songs

00:07:52.940 --> 00:07:54.493
to cover so far in karaoke?

00:07:56.670 --> 00:07:58.730
- Oh, you know what?

00:07:58.730 --> 00:08:01.240
I actually, this is a reason I have a ton.

00:08:01.240 --> 00:08:02.520
I love covering obviously.

00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:03.860
It's why we incorporated on the show.

00:08:03.860 --> 00:08:04.810
So I love covering music,

00:08:04.810 --> 00:08:07.333
but I will say,

00:08:08.270 --> 00:08:09.550
she used to be mine.

00:08:09.550 --> 00:08:10.450
The one we just did

00:08:10.450 --> 00:08:12.870
from Sarah Barelas, a
waitress, the musical.

00:08:12.870 --> 00:08:15.070
I had like heard the song before

00:08:15.070 --> 00:08:16.188
and I thought,

00:08:16.188 --> 00:08:17.021
"Oh, that was pretty."

00:08:17.021 --> 00:08:17.854
But I maybe had only heard it once before.

00:08:17.854 --> 00:08:20.000
I never actually got to see the waitress.

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:21.090
I'm never in New York.

00:08:21.090 --> 00:08:21.923
And when I am,

00:08:21.923 --> 00:08:22.756
it's there for a split second,

00:08:22.756 --> 00:08:24.220
like working the whole time.

00:08:24.220 --> 00:08:26.556
So I never had the chance to see it.

00:08:26.556 --> 00:08:29.720
And Jessie, my backyard singer,

00:08:29.720 --> 00:08:31.280
she was like, "Oh my God, I'll cry."

00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:32.379
She was like, "It's amazing."

00:08:32.379 --> 00:08:33.840
And I was like, "Oh man,
I heard the song once."

00:08:33.840 --> 00:08:35.520
And I remember thinking
how beautiful it was,

00:08:35.520 --> 00:08:37.680
but I didn't really know it.

00:08:37.680 --> 00:08:42.680
And I literally learned
it seconds right before

00:08:43.480 --> 00:08:45.160
because of how the timing of the day went.

00:08:45.160 --> 00:08:49.060
I had no rehearsal time, no learning time.

00:08:49.060 --> 00:08:53.530
And so I literally like inductor, sorry,

00:08:53.530 --> 00:08:55.849
this phone is going dead.

00:08:55.849 --> 00:08:59.000
But anyway, she was conducting me

00:08:59.000 --> 00:08:59.833
at certain parts to come

00:08:59.833 --> 00:09:00.790
because I was like, "Oh my God."

00:09:00.790 --> 00:09:01.997
I was like, "I don't wanna run this

00:09:01.997 --> 00:09:03.310
because it's such a beautiful song."

00:09:03.310 --> 00:09:04.630
And I wanted to do it justice.

00:09:04.630 --> 00:09:05.463
And I feel like

00:09:05.463 --> 00:09:06.340
when I really, really love a song,

00:09:06.340 --> 00:09:08.803
that's when I get like more OCD about it.

00:09:08.803 --> 00:09:10.795
But what's funny is

00:09:10.795 --> 00:09:12.345
I was so enthralled

00:09:12.345 --> 00:09:13.880
because I got to watch the dancers

00:09:13.880 --> 00:09:14.840
before I did it.

00:09:14.840 --> 00:09:16.290
And I knew what they were doing

00:09:16.290 --> 00:09:18.750
and the dancers, oh my God,

00:09:18.750 --> 00:09:20.600
they were so beautiful.

00:09:20.600 --> 00:09:21.930
There's just something so magical

00:09:21.930 --> 00:09:23.430
about hearing a live performance,

